WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
Pinball Quest.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.920
Once you've seen Pinball Quest, you've seen everything.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:30.080
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.140
I'm Mike.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.260
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.840
Well, that's a wrap for the show.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.760
We've seen everything now.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.680
Pinball Quest is the last thing that anybody can see.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.780
Once they've seen that, it's done.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.880
I'm actually glad I didn't read the back of the box before playing this game, because I feel like then I would have played this whole game with a trying-to-prove-it-wrong attitude.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.020
Like, okay, we'll see, Pinball Quest, just how crazy you are.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.400
I mean, to its credit, it is pretty crazy.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.300
It is.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:05.140
It is. That's why I'm glad I didn't play it with the attitude of trying to disprove its craziness, because I'm stubborn like that.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:11.180
Pinball can be just about any genre once you bring it to video game form.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:19.300
Like, in its traditional arcade form, it is a little limited, but we have seen some experimental things.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.400
However, once you make it a video game, as we've already seen on some other things, it's like, oh, now it's a...

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.540
It's a fighting game.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.300
Fighting game, sporting thing.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.840
Now it's an RPG in this one.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.820
But where's the Pinball Battle Royale?

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.220
Where's my Pinball 99?

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.480
Nintendo will get there soon.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:42.920
Where's my point-and-click pinball game, where I just drag the ball around with a mouse through the course?

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.720
Where's my shmup pinball game?

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:46.860
What about the clicker?

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.780
I guess that's just regular pinball as a clicker, sort of.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:55.360
The pinball clicker, but you have to click left flipper, right flipper, and everything, like a certain amount of times.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.520
Yeah. Okay.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.440
I like that. Only when the ball is near it, so you can hit the ball.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.940
An MMO play-by-mail pinball game.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.240
Oh. Ooh.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.920
Oh, it's almost like a...

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.880
What do you call it? Like a...

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.180
Shit, of course, I'm going to draw a blank on what this is called.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.620
When there's like a game that you got like, oh, go to these coordinates, and you'll find something, and everyone's doing it.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.880
Oh, like geocaching? Sort of.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.460
That's what I would have thought that you were talking about.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.160
What's it called? I don't know, man.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.400
Let's keep going with the episode while I silently...

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.640
Yeah, just shout it out whenever you're ready.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:30.200
I will. In the middle of the episode.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.400
So, there is the RPG mode.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.480
It would make sense to probably start there, but we're not going to do that because I want to finish with...

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.060
Yeah, I want to finish with some good steam on this episode.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:51.540
So, instead, we're going to start with the other three cabinets, which trick you into thinking that they may be like other modes in addition to that RPG mode.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.920
They are straight up calling it RPG mode, by the way.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.560
So, that's interesting in itself.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:03.180
But then there's circus, Viva Golf, Pop Pop, and you're like, oh, wow, they have these other spins on pinball, too?

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:10.380
And it's like, no, these are just pinball machines simulated in video game form.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:19.000
But they do things that a normal pinball machine can't do, which passes the video game test of why bother making this at all, right?

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:28.880
Like, sometimes we've seen pinball games that are just pinball, and that's fine if it's like on modern hardware and it's trying to be like a simulation of pinball.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:35.900
But here, it's kind of nice when it's like, for instance, the Pop Pop one, which sounds weird.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.440
I sound like I'm calling out for my grandfather.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:42.340
But on Pop Pop, it's bowling meets billiards.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.580
And there's also a jukebox that you can shoot into.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:50.380
But don't mind that, because the bowling meets billiards thing is actually pretty neat.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:56.480
There's, you're a pinball, so it naturally makes sense that you would hit down pins, right?

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:57.680
That's cool in itself.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:00.400
But then... All of these start with, you're a pinball.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.040
Yeah, you're a pinball.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:03.960
Or you're just a ball, right?

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.360
The pinball is like the entire machine.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:09.400
The ball in pinball isn't a pinball?

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.100
I think we've talked about this before, and I said it was.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.960
Yeah, I would think like a baseball is in the sport of baseball.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.400
But it's also called a baseball.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.100
There you go. We'll call it the cursor ball.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:30.480
When you knock down all the pins, which are not like, don't picture an actual just, you know, straight line bowling pinball machine.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.360
It's more like a regular pinball machine with like pins scattered around a certain area.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:48.680
Once you clear all those out, you have the ability to go down into the billiards machine, and you can start clearing off other balls inside there via this little billiards minigame.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.520
I didn't even think you needed to clear the pin.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.220
Like, I would get into the billiards thing quite a bit, actually, without clearing the pins.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.780
The, like, the nine pin arrangement in the middle, I think you have to clear.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.200
Not the other ones that are like randomly scattered.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.840
But it doesn't matter. I thought there was like just a thing you could hit that would just take you in.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.000
I mean, I got in there so often that I thought that that...

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:07.380
It was just happening to me.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.840
I'm inclined to believe what Sean said.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.680
Because I believe you have to do something.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.160
I don't think you just fall into there.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:14.180
But who knows?

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:29.200
You know, just point being that Pop Pop alone is like offering something, you know, somewhat unique to what video games can do in pinball versus what a pinball machine could do, right?

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.140
It's bringing in that video game aspect, that thing we like.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:37.800
And so, just to move along, you guys can share your thoughts eventually.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.540
But just to move along, we have Viva Golf.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:45.900
And that's the, you know, oh, now it's another pinball machine, but it's all golf themed.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:54.320
And I thought it was going to be where, like, you know, it's some kind of crazy golf game where every time you use the flippers, it, like, counts as a stroke.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.980
And you have to try to get it in a hole.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.740
And then that's, like, your score for the hole.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:04.580
And you do this through 19 courses, 18 courses, like any other golf game.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:05.900
But that's not what it was.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:14.740
Instead, it was actually just, like, a gopher cleanup game where you just try to beat the shit out of gophers until they go away and keep your lawn nice and safe.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.080
And then there's circus.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:20.200
And circus is another standalone pinball cabinet.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:22.520
But it's actually a little bit casino themed.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:23.780
There's a slot machine in there.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.840
I don't think I've ever seen a slot machine at a circus.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.220
But Joe will let me know if that's not true or not.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.840
Well, I see, I only go to casinos, not to circuses.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.100
Got it. Have you ever seen an elephant?

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.200
Have you ever seen an elephant at a casino?

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:38.800
I haven't seen an elephant at a casino.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:40.660
So I guess it probably couldn't go the other way either.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:47.780
Right. And the reason why I did that quick wrap up, if you will, of the three other ones.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.520
Yeah, you can talk now about them.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:57.980
The reason why I did that quick thing about them was just to say that, like, you know, that is, that's the standard, like, okay, PimBot, right?

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:08.060
We've played other games like that where it's like, yeah, we're just going to video gameize or video gameify pinball in a way that you couldn't do on an actual machine.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:13.160
And then there's this whole, like, RPG mode that we definitely have to talk about.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:15.380
So just thoughts in general.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:25.300
Let's not break down each cabinet, but just thoughts in general on the, what appears to be a large selection of the menu screen, Circus, Viva Golf, and Pop Pop.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:31.560
Well, first of all, it was an ARG, an alternate reality game that I was thinking of.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.640
Hmm. Good to know.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:52.980
Second of all, this is, like, this is already doing, just with these three modes, already doing, like, exactly what I, like, have been wanting from other pinball games that we've played on the NES, where it's like, yeah, it's like not keeping itself limited to the physics of real pinball.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:58.140
Like, you can, I mean, you can move your flippers, like, from the bottom to the top.

00:07:58.220 --> 00:07:59.660
That's, like, actually a big part of the game.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:05.480
And, like, yeah, your ball goes into this, like, tiny little billiards thing, and then you've got to, like, aim the ball.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.380
Like, things that, like, wouldn't really work on an actual pinball machine.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.820
And that already made me, like, interested, at least, in this game.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:24.940
Yeah, I think this is kind of doing the, like, you know, video gamify pinball in a way that's not just trying to be pinball.

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:39.560
I think in terms of, like, my favorites, like, it kind of is in order in which Mike described them, where I think I had a lot of fun with the billiard, the billiard bowling one.

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:47.320
And then in terms of just, like, theming, I really liked the golf one, and then the circus one was just kind of, like, whatever.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.060
But, like, these really are just, like, sideshows to the other mode.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:54.780
Right. But it's nice to have.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:58.640
It's nice to have, like, even though we haven't gotten into the other mode, it's nice to have it.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.800
Like, if you want that to be the main thing, it's nice to be like, oh, I want to just sit down and just, like, play regular pinball.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:11.000
Yeah. And yet, if you bought the game because it was a pinball RPG, like the back of the box touts it as,

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:17.300
it's interesting that if you went straight to the RPG mode and then said, like, oh, let me go see what, like, circus mode was about.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:20.120
And then just found out that that was just straight up pinball.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.360
Right. You might have a different experience.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:30.640
But I will say, all three of these machines are not even just competent plays or whatever,

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:40.520
but they're some of the more pure, while being video game enhanced, like, they're some of the more pure pinball experiences to be had on the NES.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:45.040
I feel like the physics don't bother me at all.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.140
Like, the ball's not too floaty or whatever.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:49.120
Things don't just, like, sit.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:52.580
There's not too many, like, pitfalls to just, like, lose your ball.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:59.760
You have that unique control of the flippers that Joe was talking about where, like, you choose to raise them up and down,

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:03.560
which is pretty unique for a pinball game and not possible in real life.

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:05.860
But there's also, like, objectives, right?

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.000
There's, like, things you should be doing, like clearing pins.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:13.980
Like, it's not just about hitting a bunch of bumpers and causing your points to go up.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:16.980
There's actual, like, things you can do on the screen.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:21.080
And so while they might be disappointing coming off of the RPG mode,

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:27.340
they are certainly giving you more than some of the other NES pinball games we have played.

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:38.720
But Sean, you said that you might have, or at least what I was interpreting is that Circus wasn't quite cutting it for you,

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:40.840
based on the order I read the games in.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:42.200
And that was the last one I read.

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:50.160
Was it because there wasn't, like, a second screen or another, like, objective thing to do,

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:56.100
and instead it was just, hey, yeah, hope you land in the slot machine for some extra fun.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:57.480
Otherwise, just hit some bumpers.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:04.380
Yeah, I mean, compared to the other two, which I thought, like, played with their concepts a bit more,

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.960
like, it just felt more phoned in than the other ones.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.760
Yeah, that's what I was, like, I agree with that,

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:15.640
because I was going to say that, like, the only, like, really interesting thing at the Circus is that slot machine.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:19.740
And then, like, that's kind of, I don't know, it doesn't, like, even really play into the rest of it.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:27.560
It's just, like, there. And what's funny is they go in the order that Sean thought they were most fun to least fun.

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:30.160
I'm not picking on you, Sean, because it seems like we're all agreeing on that.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.560
It's funny that it kind of, like, wanes down.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:36.640
Like, you start with the pop-pop, and you're like, man, I can't wait to see what the other modes offer,

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:42.180
and it just gets to be a continuously more dry experience as you go on.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:43.340
And whatever.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.360
They always say front-load your games, right?

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:47.600
Always make the first 10 hours the best.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:54.160
But to that point, it's very weird to me that the, I mean, maybe it's because of what you said earlier,

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:58.380
but, like, it's very weird to me that the RPG is not, like, first on top of the menu.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:00.500
Might as well be hidden, right?

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:01.640
Might as well be a hidden mode.

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:05.360
Yeah, like, it's a good thing I decided, like, I better play all these boards,

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:08.660
because I wouldn't have, like, I would have just been like, okay, this is just gonna be another one.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:15.240
It's cool, though, that the menu is also, like, a pinball machine, right?

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:17.940
Yeah, with that little opening, I suppose.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.340
Yeah, I mean, it's like a super close-up of...

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:22.380
Yeah, it's like the...

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.300
Oh, yeah, like the actual, like, selection.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:28.680
I was thinking of the, like, pre-menu, the title screen, rather.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:29.980
But, yeah. It's cute.

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:30.800
It's cute gift wrapping.

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:38.060
Yeah, you know, in the Nintendo games of the Black Box era, there was just like, okay, it's Mode A and Mode B, right?

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:40.980
And it was just, like, a black screen, and it had the title.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.820
And this is like, all right, I appreciate you doing some extra work here.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:49.520
And it even has the when lit ad bonus and all that.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:53.600
And I don't know who that girl is and why she's under the pinball machine.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:59.740
But she's got, like, that Roy Lichtenstein vibe going on, and I'm here for that.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:01.380
Very poppy.

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:03.600
Yep. Like pop-pop.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:07.740
Ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.140
Okay. Just end the episode there.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:11.340
Yeah, I wish.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.740
I wish, but, Joe, we have a whole mode to talk about.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:27.600
Oh, that's right. Because there is a game inside this game where most of the people who ever play Pinball Quest are going to check out, and that is the RPG Mode.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:39.100
A mode that is just, it's crazy to say out loud that pinball would have an RPG mode, but you control the pinball and progress through a six-level castle

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.320
to rescue Princess Bali.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.400
I think that's right. Bali, not Bailey.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.920
From Beelzebub, of all people.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.660
The Dark Lord of the Machine.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.140
And it's all done via the flippers.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:53.780
You're not in control of the ball.

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:57.620
That ball doesn't, like, grow legs and arms and walk around the screen.

00:13:58.040 --> 00:13:58.940
Most of the time.

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:00.120
Yeah, that's true.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.180
But the actual, like, pinball levels themselves are all just...

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:05.920
Yeah, it's just the in-between levels.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:07.860
Yeah. They're all done that way.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.200
You have to...

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:15.660
Just like in the cooler pinball machines that we've always alluded to, there are objectives on the screen.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:21.180
Usually it's just hit an enemy this amount of times, and then that means that the stage is cleared.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:28.440
But disregard that because, you know, I'm sure they could have expanded on that a little bit to other RPG tropes.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:30.600
There should have maybe been some side quests or whatever.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.220
But let's just talk about...

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:37.660
Before you talk about the pinball aspect of it all, right?

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:39.080
Like, introducing pinball to RPG.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:42.300
How much like an RPG is this?

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.780
Did they earn the RPG mode qualifier?

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:55.680
I think that the best actual description, the closest that you can get to what this game actually is, is, like, in adventure mode.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:15:05.440
Like, there are parts between, like, levels that do their best pantomime of an RPG.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:20.720
But if you're actually talking about, like, how you're playing this game, it is still a pinball game with lots of set dressing and more interactable interactions with the board.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.180
And there's, like, characters on the board.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:28.180
I wouldn't say this is an RPG in the actual sense of the term.

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:30.800
Yeah. I mean, I wouldn't either.

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:31.960
But there are elements.

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:34.580
Like, the fact that you have a strength...

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:41.720
Level. Again, maybe that's not necessarily RPG, because it's not like you level up and, like, that's your permanent strength level now.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:47.460
But, like, you do have ways of, like, sort of customizing your strength and your, like...

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:49.200
And there's a shop.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:50.760
Yeah, the shop.

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:54.680
I mean, and I guess that's, you know, buying items doesn't necessarily mean RPG.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:58.800
But, like, you are customizing the board.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:00.320
You're role-playing the ball.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:03.740
So, like, I get that part of it.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:13.480
But, yeah, I was just, like, you know, maybe back then RPG was just, like, seen more as, like, yeah, these top-down, like, these people walking around on, like, enemies on the screen like that.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:16.000
Like, that's unique.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:19.340
Maybe not necessarily, you know, not, like, exclusive to RPG.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:23.960
But that's unique for a pinball game where it's, like, yeah, there are characters that are, like...

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:29.320
It would be, like, if you were playing real pinball but you took, like, tiny people and dropped them on the pinball machine.

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:30.460
They can, like, do whatever they want.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:33.340
Like, they're not attached to the pinball machine.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:34.560
And, like, that's kind of neat.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:36.860
You have some bosses to fight and everything, but...

00:16:36.860 --> 00:16:41.040
Absolutely. And I wasn't undercutting how unique and weird this game is.

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.060
Yeah. I just think it's a bit of a misnomer.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:55.080
Yeah. I think that also back then, this especially, it's just, like, oh, this takes place in the era, in, like, the time period of, like, wizards and dragons that, like, most RPGs...

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.640
That most people associate with RPG.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:58.720
Yes. Without actually having those RPG elements.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:08.820
And knowing it's an RPG, do you think it's a mistake that it's mostly just, you know, hit enemies to clear them off the stage to progress?

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:14.360
Does that make it feel more like the action-adventure game and less like an RPG?

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:28.200
Should there have been, like, in between stages, like, a wizard tells you that you gotta, like, go up a certain ramp and do something a certain amount of times in order to learn the next magic spell or something?

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:52.820
Absolutely not. I think this is, even though I don't think it's, like, you know, it's that descriptive of a title or descriptive of, like, a marketing term, but I think that this is the, as far as they should have gotten, as far as they should have gone with this concept, I think that an actual pinball RPG would have been a real slog.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:18:01.680
Okay. That's interesting, because I do, like, I see kind of exciting and interesting ways they can expand on this.

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:14.180
Like, and I don't even know if it's necessarily making it more RPG-like, you know, but, like, saying, like, oh, to get new spells, like, it would be cool if there's, like, a part of the board that, like, is hard to get to.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:15.540
Do you call them boards? I don't know.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:16.780
I don't play pinball. Yeah, I think so.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:24.780
The part of the board that, like, is hard to get to, that, like, if you get there, you get the fire spell or something, you know, like, things like that, that'd be kind of cool.

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:26.780
And it's, like, it's maybe not even necessary.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:34.400
You can beat the game without it, but, like, there are, like, things that, my experience could be different than yours when we both play this game.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:56.480
I like the idea of, like, them adding things like this, and I also think that, yeah, I don't know about, like, doing so much more with the concept, but, like, they're very, as I'm, like, as I'm saying this, I'm kind of realizing this isn't what you were arguing against, Sean, but, like, I would like to see them, like, add a few more screens with more unique, like, ways to play.

00:18:56.480 --> 00:19:01.780
Like, you know, there's, there's the screen with the knights that you have to, like, activate and take out one by one.

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:02.800
There's, like, a boss battle.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:06.280
I'd like to see, like, more things like that, like, expanded upon.

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:26.180
I think what I mostly meant was, um, if it's, if there's going to be, like, a really in-depth, like, stat progression, or you're going to bring some stuff into, like, a, like, a turn-based thing, like, I think that would have been going a little too far.

00:19:26.180 --> 00:19:31.280
And if you had a bunch of stats to keep track of about, like, oh, how much drag does the ball have?

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:35.980
Like, oh, I mean, they already have, like, an attack power, and you kind of have, like, health.

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:39.960
I think this is, that's, like, as much as there should be.

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:45.620
Yeah, fair. But, yes, I think there could have been, like, more cool things, yeah.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:09.180
It's funny that you don't have to, like, grind stage one over and over again, the enemies on there, to be strong enough to take on the enemies in stage two, right?

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:17.000
But, Joe, you were talking about spells, which I only ever really thought of as I brought up, like, that wizard quest that I made up.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:25.640
But your suggestions are good, and I do like the idea of, like, a fire that would be maybe, like, forcibly shot in a particular direction.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:34.020
But I'm even thinking, like, why not introduce, like, some traditional pinball things, like a multi-ball spell, where all of a sudden you're, you know, you're three balls or whatever.

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:42.180
Like, that would be cool, because there is the shop where you can buy stoppers and flippers, but that's about it.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:48.500
Like, there's no additional, like, enhancements to the ball that can be done.

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:55.560
It's more like two, so the stoppers are the thing that cover the pits so that you don't fall into them.

00:20:55.580 --> 00:21:00.840
And then your flippers, there's, I think there's only two, right?

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:02.440
There's the strong and the devil.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:03.820
Is there another one?

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.020
Yeah. Just those two.

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:13.660
And those flippers just make it so that they, you know, when you use them, they hit harder than the base flippers.

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:29.900
So they're somewhat of, like, an attack boost, but there's no, like, you know, to Sean's point, it would feel a bit forced if it was like, oh, you got this sword that you now have to, like, go into a menu and equip, but you don't quite see in the level.

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:38.400
Or, like, even worse, you have to swing that sword to, like, as you're using, as you're hitting enemies or else you'll take damage bumping into them.

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:40.640
Like, I'm not advocating for that kind of stuff.

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:46.320
I just think, like you were saying, I could imagine them having even more fun than this.

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:59.880
What we got here is six, like, short stages that are prolonged because of the ability of the player to bump into these things enough times.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:01.420
You know what I mean?

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:13.440
Like, the witch on the second stage right away is an introduction and, like, you know, hey, just so you know, like, this is a game where we made a bunch of sponges and you're going to have to hit them a lot before you're able to move on.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:23.340
The witch is one thing, but then that moth lady on the stage after that or two stages after that, that is, like, it takes, like, 30 hits or something to take her out.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:25.500
Yeah, I died quite a few times there.

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:28.140
Yeah. Yeah, but you know what I mean, right?

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:39.220
It's, like, it's either just your flipper, which, you know, you can, there are tricks in pinball to make sure that you hit things at the right angle and everything, but, like, that's what it comes down to.

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:55.420
Whereas in other genres, such as the action game or the RPG, there's either the turn-based combat or the actual skill of the, like, in a contra game, like being able to dodge things.

00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:58.060
It's not really like that in this pinball game.

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:06.660
You don't quite have traditional health and traditional means of attacking.

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:10.680
It's just bumping into things like a regular pinball machine is.

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:17.960
Yeah, I guess more and more I'm kind of, like, aligning with whoever said earlier about, like, this is more of a pinball adventure game.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:21.000
That was Sean. Yeah, it's cool to me.

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:34.420
It's really cool that it's, like, yeah, there's, like, all these different unique things and, like, each board you go to has, like, different elements to it that, like, are pretty unique from the one before.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:41.880
But, yeah, they don't really, yeah, it does not really scream RPG once I, like, put my glasses on and really look at it.

00:23:42.460 --> 00:23:45.380
Like, at first, I'm like, yeah, this is an RPG because it looks like an NES RPG.

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:52.400
And they probably intended it to just be something short, fun, and silly that could help sell the game a lot more.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:56.060
Yeah. It's, you know, it's not this giant quest or whatever.

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:58.800
As I said, it's six stages.

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:05.180
But it's also just kind of, like, you know, in between you're going to the shop and the shop doesn't really have that much for sale.

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:08.820
And the way that you attack enemies remains the same throughout the game.

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:10.800
It doesn't really evolve on itself.

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:14.760
All that to say, did it hold your attention all the way to the end?

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:18.320
Did you get to Beezlebub or want to get to Beezlebub?

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:23.120
I got to Beezlebub, but I did not defeat Beezlebub.

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:24.860
But it did hold my attention.

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:32.920
Yeah, I was very, very charmed by it, especially after going the route that I went where I just went top down and I just played pinball.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:38.980
I was like, okay, Pinball Quest is just, I guess the name is just a cute name because these are just a bunch of pinball boards with interesting things on them.

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.980
And then I got to the RPG and I was like, no way.

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:44.480
Like, you know, like, this is really cool.

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:46.240
And it was interesting.

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:51.800
Yeah, charmed by it is the phrasing I would use as well.

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:56.280
Just the whole concept is cute and fun.

00:24:56.300 --> 00:25:00.820
And I think it was really clever the way they did some things here.

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:17.920
And that's really important because I think, like, contrary to what you said earlier, Mike, about the physics of it and sort of just like the feel of the pinball itself, I'd say that this kind of is lacking in that regard.

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:35.020
Like there was, I feel like your flippers are kind of, it's kind of crunchy and it doesn't feel like it's as reactive as previous games that we've liked, previous pinball games.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:44.380
And the ball itself doesn't seem to have as many, like, trajectories that it can go.

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:46.680
I know that it is like a physics simulation.

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:52.460
It's just not as crisp as I'm used to, I think.

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:56.280
Yeah, I noticed that too with some of the flipper stuff.

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:02.640
I mean, there were a couple times where, like, I would just barely tap the ball on, like, the edge of the flipper.

00:26:02.980 --> 00:26:07.060
And, like, it would either not, it would make contact.

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:11.880
Like, it would actually, like, react as if it made contact but not actually go anywhere.

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:19.220
Yeah. And then other times I would do that same thing and it would just, like, fly, like, in a direction that I would not anticipate.

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:25.080
And I felt like that, like, on the edge of the flipper was where the physics got, like, a little weird.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:34.560
But then also, yeah, I noticed, like, compared to other pinball games that, at least in the way my memory serves me, they were, like, they felt a little smoother.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:36.660
Not that it really hindered this game.

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:38.960
I just did notice it when comparing them.

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:41.240
Well, here's a good test for the flippers.

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:46.360
In the first stage of the RPG mode, after you destroy the tombstones, you have to go up a ramp.

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:51.560
How many attempts did it take you to get up that ramp?

00:26:51.880 --> 00:27:04.600
Quite a few until I kind of found the sweet spot and was able to get there pretty, because I kept falling back down once you're sort of fighting that mini boss.

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:09.280
And I was able to pretty consistently get back up.

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:11.120
But the first time, the first time's tough.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:12.880
Yeah. I don't know.

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:20.240
I actually had to, like, scrub back in this video to see what you're talking about here, but that little, like, yeah, the little pathway or whatever.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:29.440
The thing that I remember helping me is that it's sort of, if you get pretty close to it, it then, like, sort of suctions you to it and, like, pulls you up.

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:37.900
Yeah. And I was going to say, like, that's a good test of, like, because it's not a large, easily accessible area.

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:46.340
And so if it was, you know, if the flippers were terrible, right, it would be something that was very frustrating.

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:50.480
Like, oh, man, even on the very first stage, right, like, I just can't get up to the next zone.

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:56.740
But I feel like it's like you want to hit it a little later than you should in a way.

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:00.860
But once you, like, adjust and calibrate, it's not so bad.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:02.620
Yeah, it wasn't game-breaking.

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:05.720
It just didn't feel as good as previous games.

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:22.240
Did we mention that, like, at the shop you can steal?

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:24.780
No, we didn't mention that.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:27.040
Like an interesting...

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:30.680
I never tried it. I guess there's another RPG that you can be a thief, you know, the archetype.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:37.320
But, yeah, you can decide to, like, shop, leave, or steal.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:38.800
And it took me a while.

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:42.680
I think I had to look it up to, like, figure out exactly what's happening when you steal.

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:47.520
But, like, basically, I don't know if it's a 50-50 chance, but you have a chance of one of two things happening.

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:59.900
Either you just, like, fill out your inventory with, like, cool stuff, or, like, the devil beats you up and you lose half your money or your points or whatever you call it.

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:05.200
Yeah, and very, you know, that's a very basic thing that, like, they added.

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:10.060
But it's, like, it's interesting that, like, they even felt like, oh, let's add this little, like, gamble to it.

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:11.940
Did you use it often?

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:15.360
I used it once and lost half my money, and I was like, well, that was stupid.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:16.420
And then I Googled it later.

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:21.280
Lost half your money as if there was, like, a lot to buy anyway.

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:23.940
Yeah. Right. They just make those things very expensive, though.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:24.860
The flippers are pretty expensive.

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:28.520
Yeah. Until, like, later in the game, you're not going to be able to afford the extra flippers.

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:33.080
And then you just reminded me of the thing I wanted to talk about, the flippers.

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:42.600
You know, that ability to move them up and down across the stage is important in here in the RPG level specifically.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:46.100
I mean, it's important in the base pinball cabinets as well.

00:29:46.240 --> 00:29:52.860
But, like, you will have to constantly keep shifting if you're falling back into the other zones.

00:29:52.960 --> 00:29:54.480
You have to prepare for that.

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:57.800
And so do you feel like that added anything to the game?

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:01.160
It's fine if it added a challenge and that's all.

00:30:01.300 --> 00:30:14.560
But, like, was there a reason not to just have the flippers, like, be how they are in usual video game form where, like, you know, you just control all the flippers on the stage and they never move around?

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:19.760
Like, what did moving around and your control over them add, I guess is what I'm asking.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:26.180
Well, to me, it added, like, a little bit of the progress that you're making through this, like, journey.

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:35.940
But also there were times, you know, there are times where you have to sort of, I mean, it never gets super heavy, but you do have to sort of strategically think about, like, where do I want these flippers to be?

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:42.420
And there were even times where I was trying to, like, because, you know, sometimes you have two sets of flippers and they all four of the flippers move at the same time.

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:46.660
So, like, sometimes I'm hitting something from my bottom flippers and there's flippers just above it.

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:51.920
And I'm, like, trying to hit with the bottom flippers and then move all the flippers out of the way so the ball has a clear shot.

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:57.380
So I'm not, like, bumping into my flippers above, like, ah, it's a little easier to visualize than it is to explain.

00:30:57.620 --> 00:31:01.820
But that, I think there was strategy to it, yeah.

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:14.540
Yeah, I agree. I mean, there's enough of a gap between your input and the flippers actually getting there that you kind of have to be cognizant of where your flippers are.

00:31:14.540 --> 00:31:31.180
And that whole sort of, like, internal progress of each level, like, if you let it fall through to, like, sort of where you came from before, like, you kind of have to put in some extra work to get back there.

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:34.040
So it's a cool little feature.

00:31:35.140 --> 00:31:40.840
Yeah, it keeps you on your guard for sure, which is a nice enough inclusion for it.

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:43.800
And especially in the RPG mode, it felt natural.

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:46.340
The RPG mode is what I played first, so I understood it.

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:53.200
It's funny, then, when you go to the other pinball cabinet and you're, like, oh, this is just, like, that's what this game decided to do.

00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:59.180
Like, I don't think it was necessary in the other ones that you have to, like, also move the flippers around.

00:31:59.220 --> 00:32:01.720
Yeah, you could have just had flippers up there in the other ones.

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:03.460
You know, I had another set of flippers.

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:13.520
But I also think that, yeah, it is just, I think, the easiest example of, like, the developers realizing that they don't need to be tied to the physics of pinball.

00:32:13.840 --> 00:32:15.540
Like, you know, that makes it cool to me.

00:32:15.620 --> 00:32:22.680
That makes it, like, if you're a pinball fan, I imagine you'd be like, oh, that's neat because I couldn't do this in real life.

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:26.140
Like, that's what gives this appeal, you know, in my opinion.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:29.500
Did you see what happens at the end of the RPG mode?

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:32.640
Yes, I don't know if I understand it, but yes.

00:32:33.060 --> 00:32:36.880
I don't know. Like, yeah, was the magnet behind it all along?

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:40.360
Like, what are they destroying at the end there?

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:41.340
It's a magnet, right?

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:43.740
Yeah. Did they destroy the magnet?

00:32:43.840 --> 00:32:45.520
I thought they just got, like, sucked up by the magnet.

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:49.900
It looks like they got sucked up by it, but then they make it, you know, they make it explode.

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:52.380
Like, he hits it so hard that it, yeah.

00:32:52.380 --> 00:33:13.980
You know, to, you know, control a pinball, which is presumably made out of a magnetic metal, the magnet is, you know, it's like sort of evil and conniving behind the scenes, and you're destroying the thing that's trying to control you as the pinball.

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:16.240
And I don't know.

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:18.260
I like it. No, he went free.

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:22.760
Yeah, it's almost like, it's almost like there was no, like, actual, like, wizard.

00:33:23.160 --> 00:33:26.780
It was just, like, there's a guy cheating while playing pinball.

00:33:26.960 --> 00:33:29.520
And it's almost like the Lego movie, where it's, like.

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:32.560
Yeah. I don't even remember exactly.

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:35.040
Like, wasn't Will Ferrell, like, a collector or something?

00:33:35.180 --> 00:33:37.440
Yeah. And his son just wanted to play with him.

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:38.360
Yeah, right. Yeah.

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:40.340
Play with the Legos, not him.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:42.720
Yeah. No, he also wanted to play with his dad.

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:47.500
Cats in the cradle. Which is really just Lego saying, like, the people on the secondary market shouldn't be able to make money.

00:33:48.420 --> 00:33:49.680
Because they make a ton of money.

00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:51.660
They make an absolute shit ton of money.

00:33:52.480 --> 00:33:54.780
Yeah, I like your interpretation of the ending now, Sean.

00:33:54.880 --> 00:34:03.080
I see it. In fact, there's even a nice little, like, shot of what we presume is our, the ball that we were playing as.

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:06.620
He has, like, a X where, like, I guess a scar would be.

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:08.900
Yeah. He's being reintroduced to his friends.

00:34:09.340 --> 00:34:11.000
Right, right. He's like, you remember me?

00:34:11.160 --> 00:34:14.660
Like, I got captured and I was forced to be in a pinball machine.

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:17.300
And so maybe, maybe we're the bad guys?

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:18.900
Yeah, could be.

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:21.880
Yeah. I think we were the bad guys in this game.

00:34:22.440 --> 00:34:24.160
I love these credits, though.

00:34:24.280 --> 00:34:26.160
